CORE FEATURES
A. Team and feature ownership
   - Members: name, role, colour. Pre-fill the four names above; leave roles
     editable and blank. Do not invent roles.
   - Features/subsystems: name, description, status, owner(s). Pre-fill with
     PIPER's subsystems: Smart Home (MQTT), Voice (STT/TTS), LLM backend,
     Robot hardware (ESP32/sensors), Diary/Memory, Safety & Alerts, Report.
   - A "Who did what" matrix view: members as rows, features as columns, showing
     hours and number of entries per cell. Click a cell to see the entries.

B. Log entries (the core of the app)
   - Fields: date, author, feature, linked Gantt task, hours spent, type
     (Design / Build / Test / Debug / Research / Meeting / Writing), what I did,
     problems encountered, how I fixed them, result/evidence, next steps.
   - Attachments: paste or drag in screenshots; store them in an assets/ folder
     and reference them in the entry. Allow links (Git commits, PDFs).
   - Quick-add: a single form that takes under 60 seconds to fill in. Default
     the date to today and the author to the last used author.
   - Markdown support in text fields. Tags. Full-text search. Filter by member,
     feature, type, date range.

C. Gantt chart
   - Tasks: name, feature, assignee(s), start, end, % complete, dependencies,
     milestone flag. Drag to move or resize bars, click to edit.
   - Colour bars by feature (toggle: by member). Today line. Weekly and monthly
     zoom. Milestones shown as diamonds.
   - Show planned vs actual: planned dates stay fixed, actual start/end come from
     the log entries linked to that task, so slippage is visible.
   - Seed milestones I will give you after the plan is approved (one is 9 Oct 2026).
   - Clicking a task lists its log entries, and clicking a log entry jumps to its task.

D. Log book output (this is what saves us time)
   - "Generate week" button: collects that week's entries into a formatted
     log book page: date range, per-member summary, hours table, completed
     tasks, issues and fixes, next week's plan, with screenshots inline.
   - Export as .docx and PDF, plus CSV of raw entries and a PNG/PDF of the Gantt.
   - Per-member export so each of us can print our own pages for individual
     assessment. Optional: a button that drafts the narrative paragraph from
     the structured fields (just a template, no AI API call needed).

E. Dashboard
   - Hours per member per week, hours per feature, tasks overdue, tasks due in
     the next 7 days, days since each member's last entry (so nobody forgets).

DESIGN AND QUALITY
- Clean, calm, readable; works on a laptop and a phone; light and dark mode.
- Keyboard-friendly quick-add (e.g. press N).
- Import: parse an existing markdown work log into entries (I have one at
  docs/WORK_LOG_smart_home.md; use it as a seed and as a test of the importer).
- Back up automatically: every save writes data/logbook.json. Add a visible
  "Last saved" indicator and a one-click restore from a JSON file.
- Tests for the data layer and the Gantt date math (dependencies, slippage).
- Provide a README with setup (npm install, npm run dev), how to deploy to
  GitHub Pages or Vercel, and how the team shares data through Git.
- Work in small steps: build B first (log entries), then A, then C, then D,
  then E. Commit after each, and tell me what to try before moving on.

Do not add user accounts, payments, or any external API for now.